---
import { knownRelease, ZOTERO_MAJORS } from "../../../src/site/version.ts";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { BASE, CATEGORIES, categoryOptions, loadData, SUPPORT_MARKS as MARKS } from "../data.ts";

const { plugins, zoteroRelease } = loadData();
// The categories that the home page's menu offers, the only ones a link can filter by.
const offered = new Set(categoryOptions(plugins).map(({ value }) => value));
---

<Base title="About: Zotero plugin index">
  <div class="hero">
    <h1>How the index checks plugins</h1>
    <p class="intro">
      Each day, a build reads every listed plugin's GitHub repository and releases. A plugin page shows the facts that
      the build found for one release, the release the index read. This page says where each fact comes from and what it
      does not show.
    </p>
  </div>
  <section class="card" id="declared" data-about="declared">
    <h2>Declared range</h2>
    <p>
      A plugin's manifest declares the Zotero versions that it supports, in its <code>strict_min_version</code> and{" "}
      <code>strict_max_version</code> fields. The index shows that range for the release it read, after its overrides.
    </p>
    <ul class="about-list" role="list">
      <li>
        <span class="mark" data-support="all" aria-hidden="true">{MARKS.all}</span> The range covers every release of that
        Zotero version, up to its newest release.
      </li>
      <li><span class="mark" data-support="some" aria-hidden="true">{MARKS.some}</span> The range covers part of it.</li>
      <li><span class="mark" data-support="none" aria-hidden="true">{MARKS.none}</span> The range covers none of it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The index counts Zotero N as the versions from N.0 to the newest release of N. So a maximum such as 10.0.* covers all
      of Zotero 10 while 10.0 is its newest release line, and part of it once Zotero 10.1 is out. A range that ends
      before N.0 or starts after the newest release covers none of it, so a minimum such as 8.999, which authors write
      to mean Zotero 9 and newer, covers none of Zotero 8. Each day the build reads the current Zotero
      release from zotero.org, and the index keeps the last release of each earlier Zotero version. For a Zotero version
      with no known release, the index counts N.0 to N.9, because authors often write a maximum such as 10.99.99 to mean
      every Zotero 10 release. A plugin that declares no range shows no mark. The range is what the author declares, not
      a test result.
    </p>
    <p>This build used these newest releases:</p>
    <ul class="about-list" role="list" data-newest-releases>
      {
        ZOTERO_MAJORS.map((major) => {
          const release = knownRelease(major, zoteroRelease);
          return (
            <li data-major={major}>
              {release ? `Zotero ${major}: ${release}` : `Zotero ${major}: no release known, so the index counts ${major}.0 to ${major}.9`}
            </li>
          );
        })
      }
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="start-test" data-about="start-test">
    <h2>Start test</h2>
    <p>
      The build installs the plugin version that the index read in a new Zotero profile on Linux. It does this for each
      tested Zotero release that the manifest's range covers. It waits 3 seconds after the install, and then it reads one
      of four results.
    </p>
    <p data-fact="start-when">
      The build does not start every plugin each day. It starts a plugin in a tested release when the plugin file differs
      from the one last tested, when that release has no earlier result, or when the last result was not a start. It
      also starts each plugin again on one day of each week, and every plugin on a day when it cannot read the last
      published data. On the other days it keeps the last start result, so a
      result is most often up to a week old. If the build on the weekly day deploys nothing, the result stays another
      week.
    </p>
    <ul class="about-list" role="list">
      <li>
        <span class="start" data-start="started" aria-hidden="true">●</span> Started: Zotero lists the plugin as active, and
        the plugin logged no startup error.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="start" data-start="start-error" aria-hidden="true">✕</span> Failed at startup: the plugin's startup
        threw an error, or an async startup rejected, in those 3 seconds.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="start" data-start="not-installed" aria-hidden="true">✕</span> Did not install: Zotero does not list
        the plugin, or lists it as inactive.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="start" data-start="timed-out" aria-hidden="true">✕</span> Gave no answer in time: no result came
        within 90 seconds.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p data-fact="start-not-works">
      A start is not a report that the plugin works. The test does not use the plugin, and an error after the 3 seconds
      still counts as a start. The plugin's own code runs during its test, so a plugin can change its own result.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="reports" data-about="reports">
    <h2>User reports</h2>
    <p>
      Anyone with a GitHub account can report that a plugin version works or is broken on a Zotero version. The Works
      and Broken buttons on a plugin page open the report forms on GitHub. A bot checks each report and closes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The index counts a report only when the bot closed it as completed and the report names the plugin version that
      the index read. A report with the label <code>not-counted</code> does not count. Of one GitHub account's reports
      on one plugin and Zotero major, only the newest counts. When the index reads a new release of a plugin, its counts
      start over. The counts say what reporters found, not what the index tested.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="descriptions" data-about="descriptions">
    <h2>Descriptions</h2>
    <p>
      A plugin's description comes from its manifest or from the About text of its GitHub repository. For the manifest
      name and description that name a locale message, the index reads the plugin's English locale file first, if it
      has one. The site drops a description that is missing, blank, or only repeats the plugin's name.
    </p>
    <p>
      When half or more of a description is Chinese, Japanese, or Korean, the site first looks for its Latin-script
      part. It cuts the text at each <code>/</code> with a space on each side and after each <code>。</code>. It keeps
      the pieces whose letters are all Latin and that hold at least three words. If that part does not only repeat the
      name, the site uses it in place of the whole text. A piece in another language that uses Latin letters, such as
      Portuguese, shows as it is.
    </p>
    <p>
      A description override that applies to the version the index read comes first. Without one, if less than half of
      the manifest description is Chinese, Japanese, or Korean, the site shows it. If not, the site shows a GitHub
      description that it kept in two cases: the manifest description was dropped, or the GitHub description has less
      of that text.
      The Details card of a plugin page shows both descriptions.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="overrides" data-about="overrides">
    <h2>Overrides</h2>
    <p>
      When an upstream fact is wrong and the author cannot or will not fix it, the index can correct it with an
      override. An override corrects one field: the name, the description, the homepage,{" "}
      <code>strict_min_version</code>, <code>strict_max_version</code>, or the languages list. It applies to one plugin
      version or to every version. A description override can also give English text for a plugin with no English
      description upstream. That covers a description that is not in English, that only repeats the name, or that is
      half or more Chinese, Japanese, or Korean. It can also replace an English description of fewer than six words.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Overrides table in a plugin's Details lists each override beside the upstream value. Its Used column says
      "yes" for the override that applies to the plugin version that the index read. An override for that version wins
      over one for every version. The declared range uses the <code>strict_min_version</code> and{" "}
      <code>strict_max_version</code> overrides marked "yes".
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="categories" data-about="categories">
    <h2>Categories</h2>
    <p>
      The index sorts plugins into categories of its own, so that you can find plugins of one kind. It assigns them from
      words in a plugin's name and descriptions, and it adds or removes some by hand. A plugin can have several
      categories. A category can be wrong or missing, and plugin authors do not choose them.
    </p>
    <ul class="about-list" role="list" data-category-list>
      {
        CATEGORIES.map((category) => (
          <li>
            {offered.has(category.slug) ? <a href={`${BASE}?category=${category.slug}`}>{category.name}</a> : category.name}:{" "}
            {category.about}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="not-vetted" data-about="not-vetted">
    <h2>What the index does not do</h2>
    <p>
      This index does not vet the plugins it lists. It does not review plugin code, and it does not endorse plugins. It
      does not host plugin files: the download link on a plugin page goes to the author's own file, which the index does
      not copy.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
